Picture a chessboard with no edges, stretching endlessly in every direction and, in fact, in every *number* of directions. On this infinite board we hunt a lone king with a single rook -- and discover that the king can flee forever, in every dimension at once. This page lets you play with the ideas directly.

## 1. The one-move escape

The heart of the theory is a single explicit move: the king steps *away* from the rook in every coordinate simultaneously. The resulting square differs from the rook in every axis, so it lies on none of the rook's lines of fire. Place the rook yourself and watch the king escape:

